        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Panicking about the 30-minute window</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Rushing a shake within minutes of your last set is unnecessary if you ate protein in the few hours before training. Meta-analyses found the timing effect disappears once total intake is matched. Eat a protein-rich meal or shake within a few hours either side of your session and spend the energy on training instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the daily total</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Perfectly timed shakes cannot make up for a diet that is 50 grams short every day. Work out your target in grams per kilogram first, check what your meals already supply, and only then decide when to slot in powder. Timing is fine tuning on top of an adequate total, not a substitute for it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a shake in water as a meal</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A scoop mixed with water supplies roughly 100 to 130 calories and almost no fiber or micronutrients. As a regular lunch replacement it leaves you hungry and short on nutrients. Either blend it into a real meal with fruit, oats and milk, or use it as a supplement alongside food.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Taking the same small dose at every age</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Twenty grams works well for a young lifter, but older adults show anabolic resistance and need roughly 30 to 40 grams per feeding for a comparable response. If you are over 65, size up your servings and make sure breakfast and lunch carry enough protein, not just dinner.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Training fasted and waiting hours to eat</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                If you lift on an empty stomach in the morning, your last protein may have been 12 hours earlier. That is the one situation where delaying protein after training is a poor idea. Have 20 to 40 grams soon after the session, either as a shake or as a protein-rich breakfast.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it better to take protein powder before or after a workout?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most people it makes little difference. A 2017 trial by Schoenfeld and colleagues found similar muscle and strength gains whether trained men took the same shake immediately before or immediately after training. Choose whichever suits your stomach and schedule. If you train fasted, take it afterward, because your last protein was probably many hours earlier.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is the anabolic window real?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Partly. Training does make muscle more responsive to protein, but that heightened response lasts at least 24 hours, not 30 to 60 minutes. The meta-analysis by Schoenfeld, Aragon and Krieger found that timed protein offered no meaningful extra benefit once total protein was matched. Eat protein within a few hours either side of training and you are covered.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I take protein powder on rest days?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if you need it to hit your daily target. Muscle repair continues for a day or two after training, and your protein needs on rest days are about the same as on training days. Take the shake at whichever meal or snack is shortest on protein, which is often breakfast or the mid-afternoon gap.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it good to drink a protein shake before bed?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It can be. Studies from Maastricht University found that around 40 grams of casein before sleep is digested overnight and raises muscle protein synthesis, including in older men. It is most useful when you are short on protein for the day or train in the evening. If it disrupts your sleep or causes reflux, take it earlier.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I take protein powder in the morning on an empty stomach?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. There is nothing harmful about a protein shake first thing, and it is one of the easiest ways to fix a low-protein breakfast. Blending it with milk, fruit and oats makes it more filling and adds carbohydrate if you are training later. People with sensitive stomachs may prefer to have it alongside some food.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many protein shakes a day is too many?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                One or two scoops a day is a common, sensible amount for people with high targets. Beyond that, shakes start to replace whole foods that bring fiber and micronutrients, and your exposure to any contaminants rises. If you need three or more, look for ways to add protein to meals and choose a third-party tested product.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does protein timing matter for weight loss?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Mostly through appetite. Protein is filling, so placing a shake or high-protein snack where you usually get hungry, such as mid-afternoon or after dinner, helps you stay in a deficit. Your total daily protein, ideally toward the higher end of the recommended range, matters far more than the exact hour you take it.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

